{"step_index":837,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-10T04:38:59Z","input_tokens":2756,"cache_read_tokens":251073,"output_tokens":1318,"thinking":"Initial hypothesis: `index.html` might contain relevant clues based on the earlier conversation. Examining the file's initial lines.\n\nObserved: The HTML contains an instruction comment within the first 20 lines. This suggests a potential connection to the problem. Further investigation needed to understand the relationship.\n\n\n1. display\n2. margin\n3. padding\n4. color\n5. width\n6. height\n7. font\n8. align\n9. border\n10. float\n11. clear\n12. position\n13. cursor\n14. zindex\n15. overflow\n16. transform\n17. opacity\n18. filter\n19. transition\n20. content\n\n\nThe HTML files directly reveal Firestore usage due to hardcoded Firebase configuration and database calls. Achieving the desired \"encrypted local database\" effect requires an architectural shift; current implementations disclose the backend. Investigating potential methods to obfuscate or redirect database calls within the HTML or using an entirely local data store is now the priority.\n\n\nThe current HTML design inherently reveals Firestore usage, which is not desired for a fully local encrypted database solution. The core issue centers around the need for the HTML to *both* sync with Firestore (when hosted on Vercel) and function independently as a local file. The approach needs to be re-evaluated to ensure data confidentiality in both scenarios.\n\nA viable strategy involves moving all Firebase/Firestore calls into Vercel Serverless Functions, thereby removing direct Firebase dependency from the HTML files. This requires redesigning the front-end to communicate with a Vercel API, effectively acting as a proxy. If downloaded locally, the app would need to either talk to a local API or be refactored to use only local storage.\n\n\nThe downloaded `index.html` leverages AES-GCM encryption with a 256-bit key to